There, law 6. The Maggid Mishneh writes that this is explained in the Gemara there, 104a: learn from this that an unspecified statement is likewise treated as 'whether deficient or excess.' The commentators explain that this is where he says, 'a beit kor of soil that I have in such-and-such place.' But if he says, 'a beit kor from among all my fields,' he must provide a full measure without deficiency or excess. This accords with the Rama's gloss.<br>
. . . . .<br>
(A.A.) A. A.D. in the saga and Qalbin, and when you share each knee to the eye, you will find a saga as &quot;a quarter&quot;
